---
/* ============================================================================
   SingletonPage — the one renderer every singleton page will run through
   ============================================================================
   Phase 0 skeleton, written 2026-08-26 alongside
   docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-26-page-builder-conversion.md.

   WHY: the Studio's Presentation preview is only trustworthy when the page and
   the preview render the SAME template. Home already earns full fidelity that
   way (HomeBody.astro is rendered by index.astro with build-time published data
   and by the preview route with draft data, through the same query functions).
   This generalizes that contract to the other twelve singletons.

   THE SHAPE (plan, "The renderer"):
       hero            variant from the per-page HERO_VARIANTS map below
       sections        page.flexibleSections, or DEFAULT_SECTIONS[pageType]
       pinned regions  the <slot name="pinned" />, for the code-owned middles
       final CTA       from the page's own fields, unless the page has none

   NOT IMPORTED BY ANY PAGE YET. Phase 0 is render-neutral by contract: this
   file has to COMPILE, not to render. Each page starts using it in its own
   commit, verified against a pre-conversion HTML snapshot.

   Heroes and the closing CTA stay PAGE-LEVEL FIELDS (decision D2). Only the
   body converts to editor-owned sections, so there is no way for an editor to
   delete a page's h1, and the fields editors already know do not move.
   ========================================================================= */
import PageHeader from '@/components/PageHeader.astro';
import Sections from '@/components/Sections.astro';
import FinalCta from '@/components/FinalCta.astro';
import { sectionsFor } from '@/lib/default-sections';

/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Why Hero.astro is NOT imported here (found by the parity harness 2026-08-26)
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Astro collects a page's CSS from its MODULE GRAPH, not from what actually
   rendered. Hero.astro is the only hero component carrying a scoped <style>
   (the .hero-fill viewport-height rules), so merely importing it here injected
   that style tag into every page that renders through this component, including
   the text-hero ones that never touch it. resources parity caught it on the
   first compare: one added <style> block, zero changed markup.

   So the photo hero stays where its CSS belongs: in the four page files that
   actually use it (faq, contact, privacy, accessibility), passed in through the
   named "hero" slot below. The map still records which pages wear which hero,
   because that is the doctrine worth keeping in one place.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The hero map: code doctrine, not an editor choice
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Which SHAPE of hero a page wears is a design decision, so it lives in code
   (mirroring how SINGLETON_BY_PATH already works in the preview route) rather
   than as a dropdown an editor could set to something the page was not drawn
   for. The CONTENT of the hero is still every bit a Sanity field.

   Keys are Sanity document types, the same strings SINGLETON_BY_PATH maps
   paths onto in src/pages/preview/[...slug].astro.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
type HeroConfig =
  | {
      /* Home only: the two-column copy/slideshow hero. Like the photo heroes
         below, the PAGE renders it into the "hero" slot (2026-08-26, Phase 4):
         HeroSlideshow carries scoped styles, so importing HomeHero here would
         inject them into all thirteen pages. See the CSS-graph note above. */
      kind: 'split';
    }
  | {
      /* The Rule & Ledger text hero, rendered by PageHeader.astro. */
      kind: 'text';
      variant: 'rule' | 'trustLine';
      /* Set ONLY where the page labels its hero landmark today (courses,
         faculty). The other text-hero pages emit no id and no
         aria-labelledby, and adding one would be a real HTML diff. */
      headingId?: string;
      /* The page's second, quieter intro paragraph, where it has one. */
      introField?: 'pricingIntro' | 'personasIntro' | 'listIntro';
      /* The faculty trust line's field name. Corrected 2026-08-26 when Faculty
         converted (Phase 3): Phase 0 wrote 'trustLine', but the field on
         facultyPage is `aggregateTrustLine`, so the lookup found nothing and
         the hero silently fell through to the page's inline fallback. Parity
         did not catch it, because the fallback and the field hold the same
         sentence; the DRAFT PREVIEW did, by rendering the page with no trust
         line at all (it has no page file to fall back through). An editor's
         edit to that field would never have reached the page. */
      trustLineField?: 'aggregateTrustLine';
      /* About only (2026-08-26): its hero holds the headline to a 4xl measure
         and relaxes the subhead's leading. Why that two-class difference is
         kept rather than smoothed either way: PageHeader.astro's header. */
      emphasis?: 'editorial';
    }
  | {
      /* The full-bleed photo hero. The page renders <Hero slot="hero"> itself
         (see the CSS-graph note above); this entry records that it does, plus
         the choices that page makes, so the doctrine is still readable here. */
      kind: 'image';
      /** The landmark id the page puts on its Hero. */
      headingId: string;
      /** faq and contact expose a gold keyword flourish; the legal pages do not. */
      keyword: boolean;
      /** The local fallback photo the page imports. */
      localImage: string;
    };

const HERO_VARIANTS: Record<string, HeroConfig> = {
  /* Split (home), converted 2026-08-26. Rendered by the page into slot="hero"
     as src/components/home/HomeHero.astro. */
  homePage: { kind: 'split' },

  /* Text + gold rule. */
  aboutPage: { kind: 'text', variant: 'rule', emphasis: 'editorial' },
  coursesPage: { kind: 'text', variant: 'rule', headingId: 'courses-hero' },
  eventsPage: { kind: 'text', variant: 'rule' },
  pricingPage: { kind: 'text', variant: 'rule', introField: 'pricingIntro' },
  getStartedPage: { kind: 'text', variant: 'rule' },
  forYouPage: { kind: 'text', variant: 'rule', introField: 'personasIntro' },
  resourcesPage: { kind: 'text', variant: 'rule', introField: 'listIntro' },

  /* Text + trust line (the rule becomes that paragraph's top border). */
  facultyPage: {
    kind: 'text',
    variant: 'trustLine',
    headingId: 'faculty-hero',
    trustLineField: 'aggregateTrustLine',
  },

  /* Full-bleed photo, rendered by the page into slot="hero". */
  faqPage: {
    kind: 'image',
    headingId: 'faq-hero',
    keyword: true,
    localImage: 'acad-friends-talking.jpg',
  },
  contactPage: {
    kind: 'image',
    headingId: 'contact-hero',
    keyword: true,
    localImage: 'acad-table-group.jpg',
  },
  privacyPage: {
    kind: 'image',
    headingId: 'privacy-hero-heading',
    keyword: false,
    localImage: 'acad-library-room.jpg',
  },
  accessibilityPage: {
    kind: 'image',
    headingId: 'accessibility-hero-heading',
    keyword: false,
    localImage: 'acad-library-room.jpg',
  },
};

/* Privacy and Accessibility close on their statement body, with no CTA panel.
   That is deliberate (a legal page should not sell), and the schemas carry no
   finalCta fields for them at all. */
const NO_FINAL_CTA = new Set(['privacyPage', 'accessibilityPage']);

/* The literals a page falls back to when its Sanity fields are empty. They stay
   with the PAGE (each page file passes its own), because they are that page's
   copy, not this renderer's. Same D3 reasoning as DEFAULT_SECTIONS: the
   credential-less CI build has to render a complete page. */
interface HeroFallbacks {
  eyebrow?: string;
  headline?: string;
  subhead?: string;
  intro?: string;
  trustLine?: string;
}

interface FinalCtaFallbacks {
  eyebrow?: string;
  headline?: string;
  subhead?: string;
  cta?: any;
}

interface Props {
  /** Sanity document type, e.g. 'aboutPage'. Keys HERO_VARIANTS. */
  pageType: string;
  /** The singleton document (or its draft). */
  page: any;
  /** resolveSiteSettings() output. */
  settings: any;
  /**
   * Collection data the page's auto sections need (upcoming events, faculty,
   * testimonials, ...). Passed straight through to Sections; each auto block
   * reads the keys it cares about. Empty for pages with no auto sections.
   */
  autoData?: Record<string, unknown>;
  /** This page's inline hero fallback copy. */
  heroFallbacks?: HeroFallbacks;
  /** This page's inline closing-CTA fallback copy. */
  finalCtaFallbacks?: FinalCtaFallbacks;
  /**
   * The photo behind the closing CTA. Passed explicitly by the ONE page that
   * uses it (faq), rather than read off `page` here: every other singleton
   * leaves the CTA on its brand surface even where the field exists in the
   * schema, and reading it here would put a background behind CTAs that have
   * never had one (2026-08-26).
   */
  finalCtaBackgroundImage?: any;
  /**
   * Optional draft-aware fetcher for the AUTO sections' collection queries
   * (2026-08-26, Phase 4). Only the preview route passes one, and today only for
   * home, whose preview has always rendered draft courses, faculty and
   * testimonials. Undefined everywhere else, which leaves every query on its own
   * `= sanityFetch` default, so the live build is unchanged. See Sections.astro.
   */
  fetcher?: any;
}

const {
  pageType,
  page,
  settings,
  autoData: _autoData = {},
  heroFallbacks = {},
  finalCtaFallbacks = {},
  finalCtaBackgroundImage,
  fetcher,
} = Astro.props as Props;

const hero = HERO_VARIANTS[pageType];

const heroEyebrow = page?.heroEyebrow ?? heroFallbacks.eyebrow;
const heroHeadline = page?.heroHeadline ?? heroFallbacks.headline ?? '';
const heroSubhead = page?.heroSubhead ?? heroFallbacks.subhead;

/* The intro and trust-line field names differ per page, so the map names them
   rather than this component guessing. */
const heroIntro =
  hero?.kind === 'text' && hero.introField
    ? (page?.[hero.introField] ?? heroFallbacks.intro)
    : undefined;
const heroTrustLine =
  hero?.kind === 'text' && hero.trustLineField
    ? (page?.[hero.trustLineField] ?? heroFallbacks.trustLine)
    : undefined;

const sections = sectionsFor(pageType, page?.flexibleSections);
const showFinalCta = !NO_FINAL_CTA.has(pageType);
---

{
  hero?.kind === 'split' || hero?.kind === 'image' ? (
    /* The page renders its own hero into the slot: <Hero> for the photo pages,
       <HomeHero> for home. See the CSS-graph note at the top of this file for
       why neither is rendered from here. */
    <slot name="hero" />
  ) : hero?.kind === 'text' ? (
    <PageHeader
      eyebrow={heroEyebrow}
      headline={heroHeadline}
      subhead={heroSubhead}
      intro={heroIntro}
      variant={hero.variant}
      trustLine={heroTrustLine}
      headingId={hero.headingId}
      emphasis={hero.emphasis}
    />
  ) : null
}

<Sections
  sections={sections}
  fallbackEmail={settings?.email}
  fallbackPhone={settings?.phone}
  fetcher={fetcher}
/>

{/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Pinned code regions: ONE slot, always after the sections (settled 2026-08-26)
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    The few page middles that stay code on purpose render here: the courses
    catalog with its React filter island, the faculty roster, resources' zero
    state. They sit BETWEEN the sections array and the closing CTA.

    Phase 3 tested that contract against the hardest case and kept it. Courses
    reads, top to bottom: hero, "Start here" rail, catalog, editor sections,
    closing CTA. The rail became a section and the catalog is pinned, so the
    rendered order is now hero, sections, catalog, CTA. Those agree today, and
    parity proves it, because the page's sections array holds only the rail:
    the dataset has no editor-added sections on courses or faculty.

    The alternative was a SECOND slot ("pinned-before-sections") so the catalog
    could keep sitting above the array. It was rejected: it would buy back an
    ordering nobody can currently see, at the price of two ordering rules for
    every future page, and it cannot express the courses page anyway (the rail
    has to come BEFORE the catalog, so a catalog pinned above the sections would
    need the rail pinned above it as well, and then the rail is not a section).
    One slot, one rule, stated once.

    THE EDITORIAL IMPLICATION, stated plainly because it is real: a section an
    editor adds to courses lands between the "Start here" rail and the catalog,
    not below the catalog where a section would have landed before the
    conversion. Nothing on the live site moves (there are none), and above the
    catalog is the better home for an introduction to it. If a page ever needs
    editor sections BELOW its pinned region, that is the moment to add the
    second slot, with its own parity capture.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------- */}
<slot name="pinned" />

{
  showFinalCta && (
    <FinalCta
      eyebrow={page?.finalCtaEyebrow ?? finalCtaFallbacks.eyebrow}
      headline={page?.finalCtaHeadline ?? finalCtaFallbacks.headline}
      subhead={page?.finalCtaSubhead ?? finalCtaFallbacks.subhead}
      cta={page?.finalCta?.label ? page.finalCta : finalCtaFallbacks.cta}
      backgroundImage={finalCtaBackgroundImage}
    />
  )
}
